This feature would assign each property to its California school district based on its latitude and longitude. Unlike a city or ZIP-code feature, it can capture more localized geographic differences that may help explain property prices.

You can do this with a spatial join using GeoPandas: convert each property's coordinates into a point, load the school district boundary file, and join each point to the district polygon containing it.

One important detail about this particular dataset: the California boundary layer combines elementary, high-school, and unified school districts. Their boundaries can overlap because they serve different grade levels. So a property may correctly match more than one district. 
