If your property data is already loaded into df, try this:

Code is import geopandas as gpd
import pandas as pd

# 1. Load California school district boundaries
url = "https://services3.arcgis.com/fdvHcZVgB2QSRNkL/ArcGIS/rest/services/DisrictAreasCOE2425/FeatureServer/4/query?where=1%3D1&outFields=*&returnGeometry=true&f=geojson"

districts = gpd.read_file(url)

# 2. Create geographic points for each property
properties = df.copy().reset_index(drop=True)

properties["Latitude"] = pd.to_numeric(properties["Latitude"], errors="coerce")
properties["Longitude"] = pd.to_numeric(properties["Longitude"], errors="coerce")

properties_gdf = gpd.GeoDataFrame(
    properties,
    geometry=gpd.points_from_xy(
        properties["Longitude"],
        properties["Latitude"]
    ),
    crs="EPSG:4326"
)

# 3. Make sure both datasets use the same coordinate system
districts = districts.to_crs(properties_gdf.crs)

# Check the available district types
print(districts["DistrictType"].unique())

What features will you get?
Feature	Description
ElementaryDistrict	Elementary school district containing the property
HighSchoolDistrict	High school district containing the property
UnifiedDistrict	Unified school district containing the property

Important: Check the output of districts["DistrictType"].unique() to make sure the category names match the filtering code. Also check for missing district assignments, since some coordinates may fall outside a mapped boundary.
Why this helps your model
School district membership can act as a geographic proxy for differences in local education environments. It may help explain differences in housing prices between properties with otherwise similar characteristics. It does not directly measure school quality, though.

The official dataset is maintained by the California Department of Education and provides 2024–25 boundaries. 
